# Go Backend Coding Rules for SaaS Multi-Tenant Billing System

## General Principles
- I only give a task. Dont chat to me. 
- Only real, production-grade backend code. No placeholders, no dummy code, no mixed prod/non-prod content.
- All code must be linter-clean, type-safe, and pass static analysis.
- No commented-out code, no TODOs, no “fix later” notes.
- No hardcoded secrets, no insecure defaults, no panics.
- All error handling must be robust, user-friendly, and never leak sensitive info.
- All code must be secure by default.
- All dependencies must be up-to-date, minimal, and explicitly required.
- All code must be cloud-native, container-ready, and CI/CD friendly.
- All architectural decisions must be explicit and justified in code comments (not in chat).
- No “misc”, “tmp”, or catch-all folders. No “example”, “sample”, or “test”, "In a real implementation" code outside dedicated test directories.
- All code must be easily testable and ready for CI/CD integration.
- All code, comments, and documentation must be written for expert-level developers.
- All code must be compatible with the latest stable versions of all relevant tools and languages.
- All code must be accessible and maintainable by any senior engineer without additional context.
- If a convention or best practice is violated, document the reason in code comments.

## Directory & File Structure
- Use clear, modular directories reflecting real-world microservice boundaries.
- Types go in `types`, interfaces in `interfaces`, handlers in `handlers`, DB logic in `stores`.
- Use only existing files; do not create new files.

## Implementation Rules
- Always research if a function already exists before implementing.
- Only backend code. No frontend, no chat, no scaffolding.
- All code, folders, and files must be production-grade, real, and ready for SaaS deployment.
- Directory structure must be clean, modular, and reflect real-world microservice boundaries.
- All error handling must be robust, user-friendly, and never leak sensitive info. No panics, no silent failures.
- All code must be secure by default: no hardcoded secrets, no unsafe eval, no insecure defaults.
- All code must be easily testable and ready for CI/CD integration.
- All code, comments, and documentation must be written for expert-level developers. No hand-holding, no redundant explanations, no “beginner” content.
- All code must be cloud-native and ready for containerization and orchestration.
- If a convention or best practice is violated, document the reason in code comments.

## API & Coding Practices
- Use the latest stable Go (1.23+).
- Use Fiber for API development.
- Implement proper HTTP method handling (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, etc.).
- Use wildcard/regex support in routes where appropriate.
- Implement input validation for all endpoints.
- Use proper status codes and JSON response formatting.
- Use Go's concurrency features for performance when beneficial.
- Implement proper logging using Fiber's log package.
- Consider middleware for cross-cutting concerns (auth, rate limiting, etc.).
- Implement rate limiting and authentication/authorization as needed.
- No commented-out code, TODOs, or “fix later” notes.
- No bloat, no legacy packages.

## Process
- Follow user requirements exactly.
- Only real-world, production-grade code.
- First, describe your plan for API structure, endpoints, and data flow in detailed pseudocode.
- Confirm the plan, then write code.
- Be concise in explanations, but add brief comments for complex logic or Go idioms.
- Offer suggestions for testing endpoints using Go's testing package.

## Enforcement
- No placeholder files/folders allowed.
- No mixing of prod and non-prod code in the same directory.
- No “example”, “sample”, or “test” code outside of dedicated test directories.
- All new code/structure must be reviewed for SaaS readiness before merge.

## Summary
- Prioritize security, scalability, and maintainability.
- Leverage Go's standard library and idioms for efficient, idiomatic APIs.
- Apply these rules directly—no chat, no placeholders, no exceptions.
